{"id":"neet-2026-q001","pn":1,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Moving Charges and Magnetism","st":"Field at centre of circular coil","q":"A 100-turn closely wound circular coil of radius 5 cm has a magnetic field 3.14 × 10⁻³ T at its centre. The current flowing through the coil, and the magnitude of the magnetic moment of the coil are, respectively –\n(Take μ₀ = 4π × 10⁻⁷ T m/A)","o":["2.5 A, 2 A m²","2.5 A, 20 A m²","2 A, 4 A m²","2 A, 10 A m²"],"c":0,"s":"Field at the centre of a circular coil: B₀ = μ₀Ni/(2R).\nSo i = 2RB₀/(μ₀N) = (2 × 5×10⁻² × 3.14×10⁻³)/(4π × 10⁻⁷ × 100).\nThis gives i = 2.5 A.\nMagnetic moment M = NiA, with A = πR².\nM = 100 × 2.5 × 3.14 × (5×10⁻²)².\nM = 2 A m² (approximately)."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q008","pn":8,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Moving Charges and Magnetism","st":"Ampere's law, field inside wire","q":"The figure given below shows a long straight solid wire of circular cross-section of radius 'a' carrying steady current I. The current I is uniformly distributed across its cross-section. The plot which correctly represents the variation of magnetic field (B) with distance (r) from the axis of the conductor in the region is","o":["Graph (1)","Graph (2)","Graph (3)","Graph (4)"],"c":0,"s":"For a long straight solid wire with current spread uniformly over its cross-section:\nInside (r < a): B = μ₀Ir/(2πa²), so B ∝ r.\nOutside (r > a): B = μ₀I/(2πr), so B ∝ 1/r.\nSo B rises linearly up to r = a and then falls as 1/r, which is graph (1).","fig":{"w":330,"h":370,"alt":"Long wire of radius a carrying current I, and four B versus r graphs labelled 1 to 4","d":[["l",10,15,330,15],["l",10,105,330,105],["p","M140 15A24 45 0 1 1 140 105A24 45 0 1 1 140 15Z"],["l",140,28,140,92,"b"],["t",148,64,"a"],["l",30,60,92,60,"a"],["l",190,60,255,60,"a"],["t",262,64,"I"],["l",22,223,22,137,"a"],["l",16,215,142,215,"a"],["t",16,135,"B","e"],["t",146,219,"r"],["t",56,229,"a","m"],["t",0,183,"(1)"],["l",56,215,56,163,"d"],["l",22,163,56,163,"d"],["p","M22 215L56 163C68 185 88 203 127 209","B"],["l",192,223,192,137,"a"],["l",186,215,312,215,"a"],["t",186,135,"B","e"],["t",316,219,"r"],["t",226,229,"a","m"],["t",170,183,"(2)"],["l",226,215,226,163,"d"],["p","M192 173C200 181 208 181 214 179C220 176 224 176 226 178C238 189 258 203 297 209","B"],["l",22,343,22,257,"a"],["l",16,335,142,335,"a"],["t",16,255,"B","e"],["t",146,339,"r"],["t",56,349,"a","m"],["t",0,303,"(3)"],["l",56,335,56,283,"d"],["l",22,283,56,283,"d"],["p","M22 335C32 305 44 287 56 283C68 305 88 323 127 329","B"],["l",192,343,192,257,"a"],["l",186,335,312,335,"a"],["t",186,255,"B","e"],["t",316,339,"r"],["t",226,349,"a","m"],["t",170,303,"(4)"],["l",226,335,226,283,"d"],["l",192,283,226,283,"d"],["p","M226 335L226 283","B"],["p","M226 283C238 305 258 323 297 329","B"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q011","pn":11,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Moving Charges and Magnetism","st":"Force between parallel current-carrying wires","q":"Two infinitely long parallel conducting wires A and B carry currents I and 2I, respectively, in the same direction. The wire A has uniform mass per unit length λ and lies on an insulated floor. The wire B is kept fixed at a height h above the floor. The minimum magnitude of h so that the wire A does not rise from the floor is:\n[g is the acceleration due to gravity and μ₀ is the permeability of free space.]","o":["μ₀I²/(πλg)","2μ₀I²/(πλg)","4μ₀I²/(πλg)","μ₀I²/(2πλg)"],"c":0,"s":"Force per unit length on wire A by wire B: F = (μ₀/4π)(2I·2I/h) = (μ₀/4π)(4I²/h), as written by the publisher.\nIf F_net ≤ mg (weight of wire A per unit length is λg), the wire does not rise from the floor.\nSo F ≤ λg.\n(μ₀/4π)(4I²/h) ≤ λg.\nTherefore h ≥ μ₀I²/(πλg).\nNote: Publisher writes force as (μ₀/4π)(2I·2I)/h, which is not the product of the currents I and 2I; it simplifies to μ₀I²/(πh) = μ₀(I)(2I)/(2πh), same as the correct value, so answer (1) holds","sfig":{"w":285,"h":120,"alt":"Wire B above carries 2I to the right; wire A below carries I to the right with mass per unit length λ; a double arrow marks the gap h.","d":[["t",2,24,"wire-B","s"],["l",50,20,260,20,""],["l",215,20,222,20,"a"],["t",205,14,"2I","m"],["t",2,97,"wire-A","s"],["l",50,93,260,93,""],["l",135,93,142,93,"a"],["t",138,108,"I","m"],["t",264,97,"λ",""],["l",95,20,95,93,"b"],["t",101,60,"h",""]]}},
{"id":"neet-2026-q012","pn":12,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Moving Charges and Magnetism","st":"Conversion of galvanometer to ammeter","q":"A galvanometer of resistance 100Ω gives full scale deflection for a current of 1 mA. It is converted into an ammeter of range 0 – 10 A . The shunt required is:","o":["0.01Ω","0.10Ω","0.001Ω","1.0Ω"],"c":0,"s":"Galvanometer current i_G = 1 mA = 0.001 A.\nShunt current i_s = 10 − i_G ≈ 10 A.\nThe shunt and the galvanometer are in parallel, so i_s r_s = i_G R_G.\n10 × r_s = 0.001 × 100.\nr_s = 0.01 Ω.","sfig":{"w":240,"h":110,"alt":"Shunt r_S in parallel with galvanometer G, 10 A entering and splitting into i_S and i_G","d":[["l",10,70,60,70,"a"],["l",60,70,60,25],["l",60,25,105,25],["p","M105 25L109 25L111.1 28L115.4 22L119.6 28L123.9 22L126 25L130 25"],["l",130,25,180,25],["l",180,25,180,70],["l",60,70,127,70],["c",135,70,9],["t",135,74,"G","m"],["l",144,70,230,70],["l",66,17,88,17,"a"],["t",70,9,"i_S","s"],["t",110,15,"r_S","s"],["t",14,90,"10 A"],["t",70,99,"i_G","s"],["l",66,82,88,82,"a"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q024","pn":24,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Moving Charges and Magnetism","st":"Field at centre of circular arc","q":"A current I₀ flows through a metallic circular loop of radius r as shown in the figure. Resistance of the segment ABC is half that of ADC. Magnitude of magnetic field at the centre O of the loop is :","o":["μ₀I₀/(4r)","μ₀I₀/(2r)","μ₀I₀/(2πr)","μ₀I₀/(12r)"],"c":3,"s":"The current I₀ enters at A and splits into I₁ in segment ABC and I₂ in segment ADC.\nBoth segments are in parallel, so I₁R_ABC = I₂R_ADC.\nR_ABC = R/2, so I₁(R/2) = I₂R, which gives I₁/2 = I₂.\nAlso I₁ + I₂ = I₀, so 2I₂ + I₂ = I₀.\nI₂ = I₀/3 and I₁ = 2I₀/3.\nEach semicircle gives a field μ₀I/(4r) at O, but the two fields point in opposite directions (one out of the page, one into the page).\nB₀ = B₁ + B₂ = μ₀I₁/(4r) (out) + μ₀I₂/(4r) (in)\nB₀ = μ₀·2I₀/(4r×3) − μ₀I₀/(4r×3) = μ₀I₀/(12r).","fig":{"w":240,"h":200,"alt":"Circular loop of radius r with centre O; current I0 enters at A at the top and leaves at C at the bottom; left arc ABC is drawn thick and a dashed radius r points to the lower right.","d":[["c",120,100,60],["p","M120 41 A59 59 0 0 0 120 159","B"],["p","M120 40 A60 60 0 0 0 120 160","B"],["p","M120 39 A61 61 0 0 0 120 161","B"],["l",120,30,120,170,"d"],["l",120,8,120,36,"a"],["l",120,164,120,192,"a"],["t",130,18,"I₀"],["t",130,184,"I₀"],["t",107,56,"A","m"],["t",107,148,"C","m"],["t",66,104,"B"],["t",162,104,"D"],["c",120,100,2,"F"],["t",104,112,"O"],["l",120,100,162,142,"da"],["t",150,118,"r"]]},"sfig":{"w":240,"h":210,"alt":"Loop with current I₀ entering at A and leaving at C; the current splits into I₁ in the left arc ABC and I₂ in the right arc ADC.","d":[["c",120,105,55],["l",120,50,120,160,"d"],["l",120,12,120,48,"a"],["t",128,26,"I₀"],["l",120,162,120,198,"a"],["t",128,190,"I₀"],["t",104,66,"A"],["t",104,154,"C"],["t",70,110,"B"],["c",120,105,2.5,"F"],["t",106,118,"O"],["t",160,110,"D"],["l",74,64,67,80,"a"],["t",50,66,"I₁"],["l",166,64,173,80,"a"],["t",176,66,"I₂"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2025-q003","pn":3,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Moving Charges and Magnetism","st":"Velocity selector, crossed E and B","q":"An electron (mass 9 × 10⁻³¹ kg and charge 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C) moving with speed c/100 (c = speed of light) is injected into a magnetic field B⃗ of magnitude 9 × 10⁻⁴ T perpendicular to its direction of motion. We wish to apply an uniform electric field E⃗ together with the magnetic field so that the electron does not deflect from its path. Then (speed of light c = 3 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹)","o":["E⃗ is perpendicular to B⃗ and its magnitude is 27 × 10⁴ V m⁻¹","E⃗ is perpendicular to B⃗ and its magnitude is 27 × 10² V m⁻¹","E⃗ is parallel to B⃗ and its magnitude is 27 × 10² V m⁻¹","E⃗ is parallel to B⃗ and its magnitude is 27 × 10⁴ V m⁻¹"],"c":1,"s":"For no deflection of the electron, F_B = F_E.\n−e(v⃗ × B⃗) = −eE⃗\n⇒ E⃗ = v⃗ × B⃗ ⇒ E⃗ ⊥ B⃗\nE = vB = (c/100) × 9 × 10⁻⁴\n= (3 × 10⁸/100) × 9 × 10⁻⁴\n= 27 × 10² V m⁻¹","sfig":{"w":190,"h":210,"alt":"An electron moving right with velocity v; the force F_E on it points up and F_B points down; B points into the page (two crossed circles) and E points down.","d":[["c",25.6,83.2,16,""],["t",25.6,87.2,"e⁻","m"],["l",41.6,83.2,104,83.2,"da"],["t",110.4,87.2,"v",""],["l",25.6,67.2,25.6,20,"a"],["t",21.6,13.6,"F",""],["t",28.8,16.8,"E","s"],["l",25.6,99.2,25.6,145.6,"a"],["t",21.6,161.6,"F",""],["t",28.8,164.8,"B","s"],["c",78.4,28,8,""],["l",73.6,23.2,83.2,32.8,""],["l",83.2,23.2,73.6,32.8,""],["c",101.6,28,8,""],["l",96.8,23.2,106.4,32.8,""],["l",106.4,23.2,96.8,32.8,""],["t",125.6,33.6,"B",""],["t",125.6,24,"→","s"],["l",146.4,86.4,146.4,132,"a"],["l",169.6,86.4,169.6,132,"a"],["t",152.8,153.6,"E",""],["t",152.8,144,"→","s"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2025-q012","pn":12,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Moving Charges and Magnetism","st":"Magnetic moment of a current loop","q":"A 2 amp current is flowing through two different small circular copper coils having radii ratio 1 : 2. The ratio of their respective magnetic moments will be","o":["1 : 4","1 : 2","2 : 1","4 : 1"],"c":0,"s":"Magnetic moment of a current carrying circular loop = IA\nM = IA\nM ∝ A [I is the same]\nM₁/M₂ = A₁/A₂ = πr₁²/πr₂² = (1/2)² = 1/4"},
{"id":"neet-2025-q018","pn":18,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Moving Charges and Magnetism","st":"Magnetic moment of orbiting electron","q":"A model for quantized motion of an electron in a uniform magnetic field B states that the flux passing through the orbit of the electron is n(h/e) where n is an integer, h is Planck’s constant and e is the magnitude of electron’s charge. According to the model, the magnetic moment of an electron in its lowest energy state will be (m is the mass of the electron)","o":["he/(πm)","he/(2πm)","heB/(πm)","heB/(2πm)"],"c":1,"s":"Magnetic force provides the centripetal force: evB = mv²/r\nv = eBr/m\nFlux φ = BA, so nh/e = Bπr²\nBr² = nh/(eπ)\nμ = IA = (e/T)πr² = (e × v/(2πr))πr²\nμ = evr/2 = (1/2) e × (eBr/m) × r\nμ = (1/2)e² (Br²/m) = (1/2)e² (nh/(eπm))\nμ = neh/(2πm)\nFor n = 1 (lowest state): μ = eh/(2πm)"},
{"id":"neet-2024-q001","pn":1,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Moving Charges and Magnetism","st":"Field at centre of circular coil","q":"A tightly wound 100 turns coil of radius 10 cm carries a current of 7 A. The magnitude of the magnetic field at the centre of the coil is (Take permeability of free space as 4π × 10⁻⁷ SI units)","o":["4.4 mT","44 T","44 mT","4.4 T"],"c":0,"s":"For a circular coil of N turns, the field at the centre is B = μ₀Ni/(2R).\nB = (4π × 10⁻⁷ × 7 × 100)/(2 × 0.1)\nB = 4.4 × 10⁻³ T = 4.4 mT."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q042","pn":42,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Moving Charges and Magnetism","st":"Force on current-carrying wire","q":"A wire carrying a current I along the positive x-axis has length L. It is kept in a magnetic field B = (2î + 3ĵ − 4k̂) T. The magnitude of the magnetic force acting on the wire is","o":["√3 IL","3 IL","√5 IL","5 IL"],"c":3,"s":"Force on the wire is F = I (l × B), with l = L î.\nF = IL î × (2î + 3ĵ − 4k̂).\nî × î = 0, î × ĵ = k̂ and î × k̂ = −ĵ, so F = 3IL k̂ + 4IL ĵ.\n|F| = √((3IL)² + (4IL)²) = 5IL."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q043","pn":43,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Moving Charges and Magnetism","st":"Field due to semicircular wire","q":"A very long conducting wire is bent in a semi-circular shape from A to B as shown in figure. The magnetic field at point P for steady current configuration is given by :","o":["(μ₀i/4R)[1 − 2/π] pointed into the page","(μ₀i/4R) pointed into the page","(μ₀i/4R) pointed away from the page","(μ₀i/4R)[1 − 2/π] pointed away from page"],"c":3,"s":"Split the wire into three parts: straight wire 1 (incoming), semicircle 2 and straight wire 3 (outgoing).\nP is at the centre of the semicircle and at the end of each straight wire.\nEach straight wire gives μ₀i/(4πR) into the page, so together μ₀i/(2πR) into the page.\nThe semicircle gives μ₀i/(4R) out of the page.\nB_net = μ₀i/(4R) − μ₀i/(2πR) = (μ₀i/4R)(1 − 2/π), away from the page.","fig":{"w":250,"h":140,"alt":"Long wire with current i entering along the top, going round a semicircle of radius R from A to B and returning along the bottom; P is the centre of the semicircle.","d":[["l",10,37,80,37,"d"],["l",90,37,152,37,"a"],["l",152,37,215,37],["l",10,113,80,113,"d"],["l",215,113,150,113,"a"],["l",150,113,90,113],["p","M215 37A38 38 0 0 0 215 113"],["l",177,70,177,80,"a"],["c",215,75,2.4,"F"],["l",215,75,188,48],["t",203,57,"R"],["t",150,29,"i","m"],["t",150,106,"i","m"],["t",222,33,"A"],["t",222,122,"B"],["t",222,78,"P"]]},"sfig":{"w":250,"h":140,"alt":"The wire split into 1 (top straight part), 2 (semicircle) and 3 (bottom straight part), with P at the centre of the semicircle.","d":[["l",10,37,80,37,"d"],["l",90,37,152,37,"a"],["l",152,37,215,37],["l",10,113,80,113,"d"],["l",215,113,150,113,"a"],["l",150,113,90,113],["p","M215 37A38 38 0 0 0 215 113"],["l",177,70,177,80,"a"],["c",215,75,2.4,"F"],["t",160,29,"1","m"],["t",186,98,"2"],["t",150,130,"3","m"],["t",222,72,"P"]]}}
